13202 1068 #edb/ref 17592186513241 13194139535535 17592186045443 true] [17592186513202 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535535 17592186045443 true] [17592186513202 1071 "Given that $f(x)=2,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x\\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$\n- **II.** $x\\in [0,\\infty)$\n- **III.** $x\\in (-2,2]$" 13194139535535 17592186045443 true] [17592186513202 1072 #uuid "55cecc39-f460-5ecf-abe4-4ef195040d31" 13194139535535 17592186045443 true] [17592186513202 1074 "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it is one-to-one on that interval.\n\nThe function is constant: distinct inputs in each listed interval have the same output $2$. Thus it is not one-to-one on any of the three intervals.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of these intervals\"." 13194139535535 17592186045443 true] [17592186513203 1068 #edb/ref 17592186513220 13194139535535 17592186045443 true] [17592186513203 1068 #edb/ref 17592186513223 13194139535535 17592186045443 true] [17592186513203 1068 #edb/ref 17592186513234 13194139535535 17592186045443 true] [17592186513203 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535535 17592186045443 true] [17592186513203 1071 "Given that $f(x)=(x-\\frac{1}{2})^{2},$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x\\in [-1,0)$\n- **II.** $x\\in (\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2}]$\n- **III.** $x\\in (0,1]$" 13194139535535 17592186045443 true] [17592186513203 1072 #uuid "64c7ad19-8343-5b7b-a647-af679630a146" 13194139535535 17592186045443 true] [17592186513203 1074 "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it is one-to-one on that interval.\n\nThe vertex occurs at $x=\\frac{1}{2}$. On either side of this vertex, the function is strictly monotone. Including the vertex as an endpoint does not create a repeated output. An interval containing points on both sides is not one-to-one: inputs equally far from the vertex have the same output.\n\n- Interval I, $[-1,0)$, lies on one side of the vertex, since $0\\le \\frac{1}{2}$. It is therefore invertible.\n\n- Interval II, $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2}]$, lies on one side of the vertex, since $\\frac{1}{2}\\ge \\frac{1}{2}$. It is therefore invertible.\n\n- Interval III, $(0,1]$, contains the distinct inputs $\\frac{1}{4}$ and $\\frac{3}{4}$, both with output $\\frac{1}{16}$. It is therefore not invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." 13194139535535 17592186045443 true] [17592186513204 1075 #edb/ref 17592186513195 13194139535535 17592186045443 true] [17592186513204 1075 #edb/ref 17592186513235 13194139535535 17592186045443 true] [17592186513204 1076 #edb/ref 17592186408166 13194139535535 17592186045443 true] [17592186513204 1077 "I" 13194139535535 17592186045443 true] [17592186513204 1078 #edb/ref 17592186513195 13194139535535 17592186045443 true] [17592186513204 1079 #uuid "6540a663-a0df-594a-9942-b79952e1cad1" 13194139535535 17592186045443 true] [17592186513205 1080 "true" 13194139535535 17592186045443 true] [17592186513205 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139535535 17592186045443 true] [17592186513205 1083 #uuid "6ba502cf-da72-5abd-9b76-8c5384058068" 13194139535535 17592186045443 true] [17592186513206 1080 "true" 13194139535535 17592186045443 true] [17592186513206 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139535535 17592186045443 true] [17592186513206 1083 #uuid "6e2f0ce7-9862-5266-b3e7-f2842be0d59d" 13194139535535 17592186045443 true] [17592186513207 1080 "false" 13194139535535 17592186045443 true] [17592186513207 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139535535 17592186045443 true] [17592186513207 1083 #uuid "752a6a84-a836-5529-8e6b-bf3083cc174f" 13194139535535 17592186045443 true] [17592186513208 1080 "true" 13194139535535 17592186045443 true] [17592186513208 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139535535 17592186045443 true] [17592186513208 1083 #uuid "7e5fe3a2-fc6f-5a99-8c0b-cfcce4a5e79a" 13194139535535 17592186045